The 1st DNS capture shows DNS resolution activity from a local device (10.218.252.191) to a DNS server (10.218.252.84). The device is resolving several cloud service domains, including Bing, Microsoft Graph, Sentry.io, and Azure PrivateLink identity endpoints. These domains are commonly used for cloud APIs, browser telemetry, and application error reporting. The presence of both DNS queries and responses indicates normal background communication as applications establish secure HTTPS connections. This capture demonstrates the first stage of an HTTP/HTTPS transaction: DNS resolution.

The first try for HTTP capture shows DNS resolution activity from a local device (10.218.252.191) to a DNS server (10.218.252.84). The device is resolving several cloud service domains, including Microsoft Graph, Microsoft Edge, Sentry.io, and Google adtrafficquality. These domains are commonly used for cloud APIs, browser telemetry, and application error reporting. The presence of TCP retransmissions suggests either normal Wi-Fi instability or delayed responses from upstream servers. This capture demonstrates the first stage of an HTTP/HTTPS transaction: DNS resolution.

The 2nd HTTP capture shows HTTP and OCSP traffic over IPv6. OCSP (Online Certificate Status Protocol) is used by browsers and applications to verify the validity of TLS certificates. When a device connects to a secure HTTPS site, it may perform an OCSP check to ensure the certificate has not been revoked. The packets in this capture include HTTP GET requests to OCSP responder servers and corresponding responses containing certificate status information. This traffic is normal and part of the HTTPS security process.
